Path: approvals and automation — a flow built in Studio runs as drawn | 缺项 | P1

## Triage: first grade, `bug` · `priority:p1` · `domain:ui` · `area:workflow` · `pm:queue`. Direction: a rename carries the edges in the same patch, and commits only a finished, unique id

Triage seat (objectstack-wide, seat post objectstack-ai/objectstack#6015) · `session_01AavokzJ5DndAwitDXvKy4U` · 2026-10-07T19:53Z. ⛔ Not a claim, ⛔ not a dispatch.

Triage: lands in `packages/app-shell/src/views/metadata-admin/inspectors/FlowNodeInspector.tsx:477` (the ID field's `onCommit={(v) => patchNode({ id: v })}`, read on objectui `main` `d53fd02369`; the body's `:461` was at `9990f9e`) ⇒ `domain:ui`; rationale: Studio's flow designer in objectui.

- **Why p1:** a published flow whose node was renamed in Studio silently never runs that node, because the engine runs the edges as stored (measured by the objectstack-ai/objectui#11772 dev). This is the same family and the same consequence class as #11772, which was p1: a published automation that does not do what was drawn. objectstack-ai/objectstack#22088 (p1, in flight) will turn the publish into a loud refusal, but the designer would still produce the broken flow.
- **Direction:**
  - a rename rewrites every edge `source` / `target` that named the old id, in the same patch;
  - the field commits on blur or Enter, not on every keystroke, so no half-typed id is ever a rename;
  - a rename to an id another node already has is refused inline;
  - the claimant names the design in the PR.
- **Pins (as the body states):**
  - `s → x → e`, rename `x` → `renamed`: both edges follow, and nothing dangles;
  - a colliding id is refused or never committed;
  - control: editing any other node field leaves `edges` untouched.
- **Serial:** objectstack-ai/objectui#11778 (p2, dispatched) edits the same inspector region. Claim after it lands, or declare the overlap. #11772 (PR objectstack-ai/objectui#11825) has landed.
- `Clause-②: no`. Patch changeset in objectui.

